    coverageNote: "Read from Lehninger 8 section 10.2, A printed pages 346-353 (A pdf 382-389), end to end through all seven subheadings (Glycerophospholipids Are Derivatives of Phosphatidic Acid; Some Glycerophospholipids Have Ether-Linked Fatty Acids; Galactolipids of Plants and Ether-Linked Lipids of Archaea Are Environmental Adaptations; Sphingolipids Are Derivatives of Sphingosine; Sphingolipids at Cell Surfaces Are Sites of Biological Recognition; Phospholipids and Sphingolipids Are Degraded in Lysosomes; Sterols Have Four Fused Carbon Rings), plus FIGURES 10-6 to 10-15 with their captions, BOX 10-1 (Abnormal Accumulations of Membrane Lipids: Some Inherited Human Diseases, A p.353) with its own two figures, and SUMMARY 10.2 which runs onto A p.354. Page assignments were read out of A's own text layer, and the chapter's KEY TERMS index on A p.364 confirms them independently: phospholipid 346, sterols 346, glycerophospholipid 346, ether lipid 348, plasmalogen 348, glycolipid 349, galactolipid 349, sphingolipid 350, ceramide 350, sphingomyelin 350, glycosphingolipid 350, cerebroside 350, globoside 351, ganglioside 351, sterol 352, cholesterol 352, bile acids 352. Section 10.2 opens on A p.346 on the same page as SUMMARY 10.1, and BOX 10-1 occupies A p.353 in full, which is why the section's SUMMARY appears on A p.354. ONE OCR HAZARD MET AND WORKED AROUND, recorded so the next reader does not repeat it: in FIGURE 10-7's caption A renders the small-capital D of a stereochemical prefix as a lowercase a, so the caption reads as 'a-glycerol 1-phosphate' and 'a-glycerol 3-phosphate'. The running text on A p.347 is unambiguous and gives the same compound as D-glycerol 1-phosphate, so this node cites the running text and states the archaeal configuration by its relation ('the other configuration') rather than by a prefix taken from a damaged caption. SCOPE SPLIT, deliberate: the last subheading of 10.2, Sterols Have Four Fused Carbon Rings, is summarised here only as far as a reader of the membrane lipids needs it, and its full treatment — the amphipathy argument, the sterols of other kingdoms, bile acids, and the isoprene provenance — is in L-10-2-2 under topicKey cholesterol-and-isoprenoids, together with the steroid material of section 10.3. Nothing is written twice. WHAT THE CZECH NODES ALREADY HAVE, so nothing here is presented as new when it is not: 8-2-3 has the three plant glycoacylglycerols MGDG, DGDG and sulfoquinovosyldiacylglycerol with their thylakoid localisation and the acyl-chain pairs drawn in the book's figure; 8-2-3-2 has phosphatidic acid as the parent compound, the full R-group table for PE, PC, PS, PG, DPG and PI, the position-1-saturated and position-2-unsaturated rule, and the head-versus-tail amphipathy diagram with the book's own bracket labels; 8-2-3-3 has sphingosine as an unsaturated 18-carbon amino-dialcohol, the amide bond to the amino group rather than to either hydroxyl, ceramide as N-acylsphingosine, sphingomyelin as ceramide plus phosphorylcholine, its resemblance to phosphatidylcholine and its myelin localisation; 8-2-3-4 has glycosphingolipids with the O-glycosidic attachment, amphipathy without any phosphoester group, cerebrosides, sulfatides at about 25 per cent of brain cerebrosides, C24 fatty acids at up to 80 per cent, and ganglioside GM1 drawn with its five sugar units and its sialic acid. THIS NODE DOES NOT RESTATE THOSE. What it adds is absent from the Czech layer entirely: the prochirality of glycerol and the sn numbering system; the numeric net charge of each head group at pH 7; cardiolipin's confinement to the inner mitochondrial membrane and what that says about endosymbiosis; ether lipids, plasmalogens and platelet-activating factor; the 70 to 80 per cent figure that makes galactolipids probably the most abundant membrane lipids in the biosphere, and the phosphate-scarcity argument behind them; the archaeal tetraether lipids; the GM, GD, GT and GQ series; the human blood groups as glycosphingolipid head groups; cholera toxin and Guillain-Barre syndrome; and the whole of BOX 10-1 on the lysosomal storage diseases. LEHNOTE DIRECTION, flagged rather than buried: HANDOFF_LEHNINGER.md section 9f puts a lehNote on the CZECH node pointing at the Lehninger correction. The note below lives on this Lehninger node instead and points at L-11-3-1, because this session was instructed to create one new file and edit none. That is the same provisional arrangement leh_ch6b.js records; the note should be moved onto Czech 8-5-3-1 with its node field repointed at L-11-3-1 the first time ch8.js is opened for editing.",
